*.manifest
*.spec

# Temporary doc-editing artifacts
unpacked_*/
paras_*.txt
build_output.txt
build_pytest.txt

# Claude Code session data. .worktrees\ holds the per-ticket worktrees;
# an untracked one makes package.ps1 refuse to build (a dirty tree).
.claude/
.worktrees/

# AssetManager runtime files (generated on first run)
assets.db
config.json
admin_config.json
server_config.json
# What install-iis.ps1 recorded about this machine - which site and
# which app pool it bound - and the only thing _iis_host() reads. Per
# machine exactly like the two above it, so it belongs with them.
iis.json
asset_lookup.xlsx
# The SMTP password, encrypted for this machine and this machine only (ADR-0013).
smtp_secret.dat
# The external asset system's credential, encrypted the same way (ADR-0014).
sync_secret.dat
pending_buffer.json
# What the server leaves on disk while a backup or a restore is running, so a
# write arriving mid-copy is refused rather than landing in a file that is
# about to be replaced (ADR-0018). Written beside the server like the three
# above it, so a server run from a source checkout leaves it in the repo root.
maintenance.json
# What a restore copies aside before it replaces anything, one
# directory per run - update.ps1's rollback\ shape, and the only way
# back from a restore somebody pointed at the wrong backup (ADR-0019).
restore_aside/
# And what a cutover copies aside before it replaces the live pair (C4,
# ADR-0020). One generation rather than one per run, unlike the directory
# above: what is in here is a **private key**, and every generation kept is
# another copy of the one file that must never travel (rule 1).
cert_rollback/
# The Notifier's side of the same state: an answer this engineer gave that the
# server would not take yet, held until it will. Beside the app the way
# pending_buffer.json is beside the Storekeeper, and for a sharper reason - an
# answer dropped here is a Transaction the 24h Auto-Accept resolves with
# nobody having said yes.
pending_answers.json
seen_rejections.json
# The last asset types common.fetch_asset_types() actually saw, so a client
# shows that rather than the baked-in fallback the moment the server cannot
# be reached (141). Never backed up, never shipped, like client.log.
asset_cache.json
# The same shape as seen_rejections.json and there for the same reason - which
# answers this installation has already shown the storekeeper, so a decision is
# not shown twice. It arrived with the proposals work and this list did not
# move with it, so running the Storekeeper from source left it untracked in the
# repo root, and an untracked file makes package.ps1 refuse to build.
seen_proposals.json
update_state.json
# What the applier left behind about the update that brought this install
# here, so the app that starts next can write the System Log entry phase two
# had no way to write - it imports nothing of ours and there is no app left
# holding a queue (#148). Read and deleted at the next start, which is why an
# app run from a source checkout leaves it in the repo root just long enough
# to make package.ps1 refuse to build.
update_result.json
# What client_update.py puts aside before it overwrites a file, named
# beside the app the same way the files above are - so an update applied
# from a source checkout leaves it in the repo root. The rollback/ below is
# update.ps1's, on the server, which is a different directory for a
# different job and never covered this one.
update_rollback/

